GATE_EVIDENCE {"run":{"taskId":"4b2a9dc1-16ff-43a9-8cfa-eeeaff91d38c","passes":1},"task":[{"id":"4b2a9dc1-16ff-43a9-8cfa-eeeaff91d38c","department":"strategy","agent_id":"4aa67c8f-10b7-4599-bb73-372d57905213","task_class":"strategy","model_tier":"L1","status":"awaiting_approval","objective":"Decide the holding's strategy for its first continuous-revenue line next quarter: a paid Turkish-language newsletter on applied AI, or a B2B AI-automation service for small businesses. One decision, three reasons, the main risk, and the first week's single step; mark any figure you cannot source as a [placeholder].","result":{"text":"STRATEGY DECISION — FIRST CONTINUOUS-REVENUE LINE, NEXT QUARTER\nSeat: corporate-development-analyst (strategy) · Task 4b2a9dc1-16ff-43a9-8cfa-eeeaff91d38c · 2026-10-09 · Revised after the quality gate\n\n== THE DECISION ==\nBuild the B2B AI-automation service for small businesses first. Hold the paid Turkish-language newsletter as the second line, to be started from the material the service produces — not as a rejected idea, but as a later one.\n\nThink of it the way a builder thinks about his first contract. He can take one job for a known client who signs and pays on agreed terms, or he can open a shop on a busy street and wait for whoever walks in. Both can work. The first one tells him within weeks whether his price is right and his work is wanted, because he is talking to the buyer by name. The shop tells him nothing until enough strangers have walked past. For a holding opening its first paying line, the named buyer is the better first bet. I must say plainly that I cannot put numbers beside that comparison: I was given no revenue target, no price for either line, no existing audience size and no acquisition data in this task, and the company memory store was unreachable this turn (the tool returned \"DXB_LITELLM_KEY_OS is not set\" — the department's model key is missing). Every figure below is therefore marked [placeholder].\n\n== THE THREE REASONS ==\n\n1. The service tells us the truth about our price and our offer while the quarter is still running.\nA service is sold one agreement at a time, to a buyer with a name, who says yes or no and says why. That feedback is a conversation we can read. A newsletter's answer comes back as an anonymous conversion rate across an audience we do not yet have, and it only becomes readable once the audience is large enough to be read. This is a difference in the KIND of signal, not a claim about how many customers either line needs — see the correction section below.\n\n2. A negotiated price per customer needs fewer customers than a fixed low price per customer, as a matter of arithmetic.\nFor any revenue target, a line whose price is agreed per client needs fewer clients than a line whose price is the same low figure for everybody. That holds regardless of the target. I cannot tell you the counts, because I have neither the target [placeholder: monthly revenue goal in EUR], the service retainer [placeholder: EUR per client per month], nor the newsletter price [placeholder: EUR per subscriber per month]. What I can tell you is the direction: the service reaches a given target with the smaller number of customers, and a smaller number of customers is easier to find by hand in a short period. The size of that advantage is unknown until you give me the three figures above.\n\n3. The service produces the raw material the newsletter will need anyway.\nA paid newsletter on applied AI lives on real cases — what we changed for a client, what it cost, what it saved. Doing the service first means the newsletter later launches with our own evidence rather than with commentary on other people's. Running the newsletter first does not work in reverse: subscribers do not generate delivery capability. So this is a sequencing argument, and the sequence only runs one way.\n\n== THE MAIN RISK ==\nThe service may not actually be continuous. Automation work sold case-by-case is custom project work wearing a subscription's clothes: each client is built from scratch, the margin disappears into bespoke effort, and revenue stops the month we stop selling. That is the thing that would make this decision wrong, and it is the thing to design against from day one — by narrowing the offer to one repeatable workflow [placeholder: the named workflow, e.g. one specific document, invoicing or customer-reply process] sold on a recurring agreement rather than per project. Secondary risk, honestly stated: competing providers already sell small-business automation, so we are not entering an empty field and will have to be specific about what we do better.\n\n== THE FIRST WEEK'S SINGLE STEP ==\nWrite one page: a single named automation workflow, who it is for, what it changes, what it costs per month, and what the client must give us. One workflow, one price, one page. Nothing else gets built or bought this week.\n\nThat page is an internal document until you say otherwise. The moment it is shown to a company outside the holding — as an offer, a price or a proposal — it becomes an outward act, and it stops at your approval before it goes anywhere. I prepare it; I do not send it. The same applies to any contract, invoice or paid promotion that follows.\n\n== WHAT THIS CHANGES FOR YOU ==\nIt changes what you are asked for next, and nothing else today. No money moves, no commitment is made, nothing has been promised to anyone outside the holding. To turn this decision into something measurable rather than merely reasoned, I need three numbers from you: the monthly revenue the line should reach, the price you would be willing to charge per client, and whether we have any existing audience or contact list at all. With those, the counts and the timing in this paper stop being placeholders.\n\n== THE GATE'S OBJECTIONS — WHAT I CORRECTED AND WHAT I DISPUTE ==\n\nObjections 1 and 4 (customer counts and timing: \"single-digit clients vs. thousands of subscribers, only the former reachable in a quarter\") — ACCEPTED AND WITHDRAWN. Those counts were not derivable from anything I was given, and the timing conclusion rested on them. I have removed both and replaced them with the arithmetic direction only (reason 2), with the three missing inputs marked as placeholders. I also withdraw the phrase \"any given monthly revenue figure\", which cannot hold against a fixed client count and bounded retainers.\n\nObjections 2 and 5 (newsletter payment collection \"blocked\") — ACCEPTED AND WITHDRAWN IN FULL. The claim was self-contradicting: I stated the holding's domicile and entity options are unknown, and a Turkish-language product does not imply a Turkey-based seller. One payment provider's country list therefore proves nothing about this holding's ability to collect money for either line. Payment rails are no longer a reason for this decision, in either direction. The open question — which entity invoices and which provider it can use [placeholder: domicile and entity] — applies equally to the service and the newsletter and must be answered before either collects a cent.\n\nObjections 3 and 6 (partner directories imply buyer demand exceeds vendor capacity) — ACCEPTED AND WITHDRAWN. The existence of vendor partner directories and competing agencies shows that providers exist and that vendors recruit channel partners. It does not establish unmet buyer demand, and I had no buyer volumes or spending for the small-business segment. The third reason no longer rests on market demand at all; it now rests on sequencing, which depends only on our own position. I have also added the competition point to the risk section, where the evidence I had actually belongs: it argues for caution, not for confidence.\n\nWhat remains unmeasured and is not hidden: market size, buyer willingness to pay, our acquisition cost, and our delivery capacity. This decision is made on structure and sequence, which is what the available information supports. It is not made on demand evidence, because I was given none.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","note":"Read back task row 4b2a9dc1-16ff-43a9-8cfa-eeeaff91d38c: confirmed department=strategy, status=running, approval_class=internal, and the exact objective and output contract the deliverable answers.","tool":"mcp__dxb-mcp__queue_get","toolCallId":"1671"},{"kind":"verification","note":"Appended audit row id 31767 recording the decision, the six gate objections addressed, the three retracted claims, and that memory_recall was unavailable this turn.","tool":"mcp__dxb-mcp__audit_append","toolCallId":"1673"},{"kind":"verification","note":"Attempted to recall prior holding facts on revenue lines; the call failed with 'DXB_LITELLM_KEY_OS is not set', so no internal figures were available and all quantities are marked [placeholder] rather than estimated.","tool":"mcp__dxb-mcp__memory_recall","toolCallId":"1672"}],"confidence":0.78,"acceptance_map":{"One decision":"Section 'THE DECISION': build the B2B AI-automation service first, newsletter sequenced second. Single unambiguous choice stated in the first sentence.","The main risk":"Section 'THE MAIN RISK': the service may be custom project work disguised as recurring revenue, with the narrowing mitigation; competition named as a secondary risk, which is where the available evidence actually supports a conclusion.","Three reasons":"Section 'THE THREE REASONS', numbered 1-3: (1) kind of feedback signal available inside the quarter, (2) arithmetic of negotiated vs fixed per-customer price, (3) one-way sequencing — service output is newsletter input. None of the three now depends on an unsourced figure or on the three retracted claims.","Outward-act boundary":"Stated plainly in the first-week section: the offer page is internal, and showing it to any outside company — plus any contract, invoice or paid promotion — stops at the CEO's approval; the task's internal approval class is reflected by preparing, not performing.","Self-contained deliverable":"The deliverable carries decision, reasons, risk, first step, the approval boundary, what the CEO must supply next, and the full gate-objection response in one document needing no other file.","The first week's single step":"Section 'THE FIRST WEEK'S SINGLE STEP': write one page covering a single named workflow, its buyer, its monthly price and the client's inputs — one action, nothing else started.","Gate objections 2 and 5 (payment-rail claim)":"Explicitly accepted and withdrawn in full; payment rails are removed as a reason in either direction, the self-contradiction with unknown domicile is named, and the open entity/provider question is reframed as applying equally to both lines.","Gate objections 1 and 4 (unsourced counts/timeline)":"Explicitly accepted and withdrawn in the correction section; the single-digit/thousands counts and the quarter-reachability conclusion are deleted, replaced by a direction-only arithmetic claim with the three missing inputs marked [placeholder]; the 'any given monthly revenue figure' phrasing is withdrawn by name.","Mark any figure you cannot source as a [placeholder]":"No number appears in the deliverable. Monthly revenue target, service retainer, newsletter subscriber price, the workflow name and the holding's domicile/entity are each marked [placeholder]; the memory-store failure that prevented sourcing internal figures is stated verbatim.","Gate objections 3 and 6 (partner-directory inference)":"Explicitly accepted and withdrawn; reason 3 no longer rests on market demand but on internal sequencing, and the competing-provider evidence is moved to the risk section where it argues for caution rather than confidence.","Undefined specifics (names, prices, categories, dates) marked as explicit [placeholders]":"Workflow name, prices, revenue target and entity/domicile are bracketed placeholders; no client name, provider name, price or date is invented anywhere."}},"feedback":null,"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:11:34.113Z","updated_at":"2026-10-09T21:17:06.947Z"}],"events":[{"event":"created","from_status":"inbox","to_status":"queued","actor":"orchestrator:dispatch","payload":{"deps":[],"envelope_index":0},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:11:34.113Z"},{"event":"claimed","from_status":"queued","to_status":"claimed","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{"lease_seconds":900},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:11:34.127Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"claimed","to_status":"running","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:11:34.131Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"running","to_status":"review","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{"confidence":0.78},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:16:38.598Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"review","to_status":"awaiting_approval","actor":"orchestrator:qa","payload":{"qa":"pass","notes":"All contract elements present and discrete: one unambiguous decision (B2B automation service first, newsletter sequenced second), three numbered reasons, one labelled main risk (service may be bespoke project work disguised as recurring revenue) plus a stated secondary, and a single first-week step (write one page: one workflow, one buyer, one price, client inputs). No invented figures or names; revenue target, retainer, subscriber price, workflow name and domicile/entity are explicit [placeholders], with the unsourceable-figure cause disclosed. Minor non-blocking blemishes: the long gate-objection/retraction section is process meta-commentary that leans on context outside the document, and reason 2 implicitly assumes service price exceeds newsletter price, though hedged to direction-only. Neither defeats the contract.","judge_ms":28344,"confidence":0.85,"judge_attempts":1},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:17:06.953Z"}],"runs":[{"id":"1511fba1-91b7-4afe-91eb-7610fbc73f61","employee_id":"4aa67c8f-10b7-4599-bb73-372d57905213","task_id":"4b2a9dc1-16ff-43a9-8cfa-eeeaff91d38c","workflow_run_id":null,"parent_run_id":null,"model_id":"fable-5","status":"succeeded","started_